>>In the most general terms, is there such a thing as a hopeless character.

Yes, there is. And it may happen quite early.

>>That is, suppose a character through some series of misfortunes manages to
>>find himself "well and truly [screwed]" in the game, is there a set of
>>circumstances where you would just decide that the character is a writeoff
>>and #quit, or is a character in even the most hopeless-seeming set of
>>circumstances still able to ascend?

One scenario; you get isolated from your pet falling down a trap door a
couple of levels. Landing in the dark. Falling deeper while searching the
upstairs. Get surrounded by a crowd of creatures (soldier ants, e.g.).
Eventually you fail to write The Word. (Been there, done that.)

That is just one of many possible scenarios.

And there is no (quasi-)formal criterion to specify a hopeless character.

With increasing (RL-)experience you learn to know what situations may get
hazardous. Then you start to prepare yourself to reduce the overall risk.
Nonetheless, you can't guarantee an ascension, even if you play without
doing any stupid move.

